{"state": {"case_id": "MB_13", "timestep": 3, "case_summary": "A 55-year-old man presents to his primary care physician with fatigue, malaise, and a painful rash. The patient states that his symptoms have been worsening over the past week. He also has experienced abdominal pain and diarrhea. He has a medical history of obesity and smokes regularly. His temperature is 97.5°F (36.4°C), blood pressure is 142/82 mmHg, pulse is 85/min, respirations are 15/min, and oxygen saturation is 98% on room air. Physical exam is notable for an erythematous rash with papules and plaques on the patient's face, torso, and extremities. He states that the rash is painful. A fingerstick blood glucose is unable to accurately read his blood glucose and gives a reading of > 500 mg/dL. Which of the following is the most likely underlying pathophysiology of this patient’s condition?\n\nOptions:\nA. Alpha cell tumor\nB. Beta cell destruction\nC. Beta cell tumor\nD. Hypercortisolism\nE. Insulin resistance\n", "root": {"label": "Subacute Systemic Illness with Widespread Painful Erythematous Rash and Hyperglycemia (Suspected Metabolic or Infectious Aetiology)", "time_course": "subacute", "severity": "unspecified", "confidence": 0.6, "supporting_facts": ["Painful erythematous rash with papules/plaques involving face, torso, and extremities", "Systemic symptoms of fatigue, malaise, abdominal pain, and diarrhea", "Hyperglycemia (fingerstick BG >500 mg/dL)", "Progressive worsening over 1 week"], "excluded_candidates": ["Autoimmune vasculitis: less likely due to absence of neutrophilia or specific lab markers", "Viral exanthem: possible but ranked lower due to dominant hyperglycemia and constitutional symptoms", "Diabetic dermopathy variant: less likely given the acute/painful presentation rather than chronic non-painful lesions"], "alarm_features": ["Hyperglycemia >500 mg/dL", "Systemic constitutional symptoms (fatigue, malaise)", "Gastrointestinal involvement (abdominal pain, diarrhea)"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Endocrine Tumor with Cutaneous Eruption", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.18083462132921174, "posterior": 0.07725200440182362, "danger": 0.8, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["Painful rash with systemic symptoms is less typical of endocrine tumor eruptions but doesn't exclude them entirely."], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B1.1", "B1.2"], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.5, "interrupt_relevance": 0.7, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "etiology", "representative_diseases": []}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Adrenal Hyperfunction with Systemic Features", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "reopened", "prior": 0.07727975270479136, "posterior": 0.04401823612639523, "danger": 0.7, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": ["Painful rash with systemic symptoms is less typical of endocrine tumor eruptions but doesn't exclude them entirely."], "evidence_against": ["Lack of classic Cushingoid features strongly reduces the likelihood of adrenal hyperfunction."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.5, "interrupt_relevance": 0.7, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "mechanism", "representative_diseases": []}, "B3": {"id": "B3", "label": "Severe Insulin Resistance with Metabolic Complications", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.3863987635239567, "posterior": 0.22637950007860402, "danger": 0.5, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": ["Absence of acanthosis nigricans weakly reduces support for severe insulin resistance as the primary mechanism."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B3.1", "B3.2", "B3.3"], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.3, "interrupt_relevance": 0.3, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "risk_context", "representative_diseases": []}, "B4": {"id": "B4", "label": "Beta Cell Dysfunction (Type 1/Type 2/Secondary)", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.0, "posterior": 0.28523817009904107, "danger": 0.6, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["Age and obesity are classic risk factors for type 2 diabetes, moderately supporting beta cell dysfunction as the primary mechanism."], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B4.1", "B4.2", "B4.3"], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.4, "interrupt_relevance": 0.5, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "mechanism", "representative_diseases": []}, "B5": {"id": "B5", "label": "Reactive/Non-malignant Hyperglycemia with Cutaneous Involvement", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "reopened", "prior": 0.07727975270479136, "posterior": 0.04401823612639523, "danger": 0.4, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.3, "interrupt_relevance": 0.4, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "other", "representative_diseases": []}, "B1.1": {"id": "B1.1", "label": "Glucagonoma (Alpha Cell Tumor) with Necrolytic Migratory Erythema", "level": 2, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.069, "danger": 0.8, "parent": "B1", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["The painful rash morphology differs from typical glucagonoma rash, but this is not definitive exclusion due to variable presentations."]}, "B1.2": {"id": "B1.2", "label": "Insulinoma (Beta Cell Tumor) with Paraneoplastic Rash", "level": 2, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.009, "danger": 0.5, "parent": "B1", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Hyperglycemia directly contradicts insulinoma's hypoglycemic signature, strongly excluding this diagnosis."]}, "B3.1": {"id": "B3.1", "label": "Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus with Severe Insulin Resistance", "level": 2, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.151, "danger": 0.5, "parent": "B3", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}, "B3.2": {"id": "B3.2", "label": "Acquired Lipodystrophy Syndrome with Hyperglycemia", "level": 2, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.05, "danger": 0.6, "parent": "B3", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Systemic symptoms are atypical for acquired lipodystrophy, which primarily presents with localized fat loss and eruptive xanthomas."]}, "B3.3": {"id": "B3.3", "label": "Genetic Lipodystrophy Syndrome (e.g., Berardinelli-Seip, Dunn)", "level": 2, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.025, "danger": 0.7, "parent": "B3", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Congenital fat distribution abnormalities are required for genetic lipodystrophy, making this diagnosis unlikely in a 55-year-old with acquired obesity."]}, "B4.1": {"id": "B4.1", "label": "Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus with Beta Cell Dysfunction", "parent": "B4", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.17114290205942465, "posterior": 0.17114290205942465, "danger": 0.5, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.3, "interrupt_relevance": 0.3, "level_role": "specific_disease", "classification_axis": "etiology", "representative_diseases": []}, "B4.2": {"id": "B4.2", "label": "Type 1 Diabetes Mellitus with Autoimmune Beta Cell Destruction", "parent": "B4", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.05704763401980822, "posterior": 0.05704763401980822, "danger": 0.7, "actionability": 0.0, "explanato