cell dysfunction as the underlying mechanism?", "summary": ""}, {"t": 3, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the patient's lack of classic Cushingoid features (moon face, buffalo hump) rule out adrenal hyperfunction with systemic features as a dangerous alternative, given these are hallmark findings in…", "summary": ""}, {"t": 3, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of documented acute infection or stress in the patient's history argue against reactive hyperglycemia with cutaneous involvement as the underlying mechanism?", "summary": ""}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": false, "reason": "Patient remains hemodynamically stable with no evidence of airway compromise, respiratory failure, or end-organ signs of shock. Hyperglycemia >500 mg/dL is present but lacks overt DKA features (e.g., Kussmaul breathing, altered mental status). The rash and systemic symptoms are subacute with no acute life-threatening features.", "required_actions": []}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": "Leading branch (B4: Beta Cell Dysfunction) posterior is 0.285 < 0.75. Dangerous alternative B1.1 (Glucagonoma) has posterior 0.069 < 0.10, but B3.1 (Type 2 DM) remains unresolved. No depth limit reached (current level 2/3). Further testing/discrimination is needed to resolve uncertainty between B4.1 (Type 2 DM) and B3.1 (Severe Insulin Resistance), which share overlapping features but differ in pathophysiology and management implications."}, "turn_budget_used": 2, "estimated_remaining_value": 1.0, "max_turn_budget": 5, "latest_action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "diagnosis_readiness_score": 0.17114290205942465, "benchmark_output_ready": false, "static_question": "Which of the following is the most likely underlying pathophysiology of this patient’s condition?", "static_options": [{"id": "A", "description": "Alpha cell tumor"}, {"id": "B", "description": "Beta cell destruction"}, {"id": "C", "description": "Beta c